+Remote monitoring is opt-in. Configure an SSH host alias in `~/.ssh/config` for the Linux account that owns the agent
+logs, including its user, port and identity as needed. Use normal SSH host-key verification, pre-trust the host key,
+and use noninteractive key or SSH agent authentication. Enable each host in Settings → Sources. Local sources and all
+enabled remote sources appear together in the native dashboard; source identity includes the host and source kind.
+Settings → Sources shows each host's online, offline or disabled status and its last connection error until a successful
+snapshot arrives.
+Local diagnostic reports and live reporting contain local data only, not remote data. Remote SSH is independent of the
+network switch: disable the host to stop it. The app selects its bundled collector for the host architecture, uploads
+it over SSH and runs it over SSH standard streams. There is no HTTP service, listening port, Python runtime or sudo.
 
 The Linux collector reuses the native measurement core, including omp parsing and parent/child deduplication. The
 remote host has its own identity and history, separate from local usage. A disconnected host is shown offline with
 stale data, not as idle or zero usage; reconnection replaces its snapshot and history rather than adding them again.
 On macOS, cached history remains stale after reconnecting until a new history frame arrives, even if live snapshots resume.
+When multiple connections report the same machine, history is selected per source; stale or partial status reflects every connection whose history contributes.
 Fields absent from logs are unknown. Live rates inferred from recent completed responses are estimates; completed
 response rates use the logged duration. In particular omp commonly writes a response when it completes, so remote
 monitoring cannot measure per-token streaming speed or infer an unrecorded time to first token. End-to-end speed includes
 the first-token wait. It cannot reveal router retries or account cooldowns absent from the logs.
 
-The remote dashboard shows live rates, sources, agents, today's statistics and a history entry when available. Remote
+Remote sources and agents appear alongside local sources in the native dashboard and history when available. Remote
 data does not include categorized today's token totals, project paths, traffic or a continuous live chart; unavailable
-values, including missing price data, remain unknown.
-History dates follow the remote host's local time zone.
-The history window follows the selected target. If an older collector lacks the remote date or complete daily
-breakdown, that history is unavailable and cannot be exported. Partial log reads are marked as potentially incomplete;
-network and performance history are not available for remote targets.
+values, including missing price data, remain unknown. History is aggregated in the viewer's time zone, requested by
+the client from the collector. Partial log reads are marked as potentially incomplete. Remote network and performance
+history are unavailable; disconnected intervals remain gaps rather than continuous measurements.
+After the client requests its time zone, snapshot today totals also use that zone. Untagged or null-zone snapshots
+retain collector-local compatibility behavior. History from older collectors without a matching time-zone tag is
+unavailable rather than being mislabeled as viewer-local.
 
 The collector keeps a private history cache on the Linux host at `~/.cache/agentspeed-collector/history.sqlite3`.
 An exclusive lock allows one collector to use it; concurrent collectors or an unsafe or unavailable cache use memory
